      {
        "canonical_field": "essay:generated:7f47df13-6fac-4df1-b1b5-801c2b080879",
        "expected": "What draws me to Applied Intuition is that the work is tools and infrastructure for systems that have to function in the physical world, where a failure is not a bad demo but a real consequence. That is the kind of problem I keep choosing. I built a Playwright-based job-application agent where every mutating capability sits behind fail-closed flags and every application moves through an explicit state machine, because I did not want a single wrong browser action to submit something real. I also built a validation ladder so a capability is not called working until it has been tested against the level of reality it claims to support, not just a local fixture. At Summer Atlantic Capital I work on the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection system, including temporal sequence modeling over event streams and a learning-to-rank layer, and the hardest part has been operational usefulness rather than raw model performance. Applied Intuition seems to take that distinction seriously across simulation, autonomy, and the infrastructure eighteen of the top twenty automakers depend on. I want to work on systems where the boundary between machine action and human judgment is designed rather than assumed.",
        "observed": "What draws me to Applied Intuition is that the work is tools and infrastructure for systems that have to function in the physical world, where a failure is not a bad demo but a real consequence. That is the kind of problem I keep choosing. I built a Playwright-based job-application agent where every mutating capability sits behind fail-closed flags and every application moves through an explicit state machine, because I did not want a single wrong browser action to submit something real. I also built a validation ladder so a capability is not called working until it has been tested against the level of reality it claims to support, not just a local fixture. At Summer Atlantic Capital I work on the machine-learning core of an anomaly-detection system, including temporal sequence modeling over event streams and a learning-to-rank layer, and the hardest part has been operational usefulness rather than raw model performance. Applied Intuition seems to take that distinction seriously across simulation, autonomy, and the infrastructure eighteen of the top twenty automakers depend on. I want to work on systems where the boundary between machine action and human judgment is designed rather than assumed.",
